/**

@page documentation_exahype2_solvers_finite_volumes Finite Volume solver

\tableofcontents


ExaHyPE 2 offers a range of Finite Volume solvers. They are designed to be
stand-alone solvers, but nothing stops you from using them as a posteriori
limiters by coupling them to higher order schemes.
Some hands-on descriptions of the numerics can be found in @ref page_tutorials_exahype2_numerics_higher_order_finite_volumes "the tutorial section".


ExaHyPE's Finite Volume scheme is based upon block-structured Cartesian meshes.
All Finite Volume solvers work with blockstructured AMR: They embed @f$ p \times p@f$ patches
into the octants (cells of the spacetree). To avoid confusion, we use the
term volume for the cells within the patch, as cell would be ambiguous
given that we construct the host mesh from spacetree cells.


@image html documentation/exahype2/solvers/block-structured.png

The solver family dispatches over the cells of the partition, one patch per cell.
Per patch, it calls an ExaHyPE update kernel, which takes the patch and moves it forwards in time by a given @f$ \Delta t @f$.
ExaHyPE is shipped with several update schemes. You can also write your own
numerical scheme manually.

## Storage scheme

Every patch depends upon its neighbouring, i.e. face-connected patches.
To be able to do this, ExaHyPE passes a @f$ (p+2N)^d @f$ patch into a kernel, where @f$ p @f$ is the number of volumes per patch, @f$ d @f$ is the dimension,
and @f$ N \geq 1 @f$ is halo size.
Different Finite Volume schemes have different halo sizes.
You may assume that ExaHyPE fills these halo values with valid data from the neighbours, i.e. their synchronisation is none of the kernel's business.
The input is called @f$ Q_{in} @f$ from hereon.
The kernel returns a @f$ (p+2 N)^d @f$ with the new time step's data, called @f$ Q_{out} @f$.


Here are some details about the data used within the kernels:

- The halo data, i.e. the @f$ N @f$ layer around the patch is automatically filled.
  However, we only maintain the face-connected data. Consequently, the diagonal (corners)
  do not exist. To make the whole logic easier, the @f$ Q_{in} @f$ nevertheless hold these data.
  They contain garbage. Example: In a @f$ 7 \times 7 @f$ patch world with @f$ N=2 @f$, we have
  49 volumes in the core centre, surrounded by @f$ 2d \cdot (N \cdot p^{d-1}) = 4 \cdot (2 \cdot 7) @f$ face-connected values.
  The @f$ 4 \cdot (2 \cdot 2 ) @f$ corners of the halo do not hold any meaningful data.
  Nevertheless, we let @f$ Q_{in} @f$ hold @f$ (7+2\cdot 2)^2 @f$ volumes.
- The volumes within @f$ Q_{in} @f$ and @f$ Q_{out} @f$ are ordered lexicographically.
- The data are stored as an array of structs (AoS).
- If a PDE is described by @f$ K_{pde} @f$  real values subject to the wave equations and @f$ K_{aux} @f$ auxiliary variables
  (material parameters), then the @f$ Q_{in} @f$ contains @f$ K_{pde} + K_{aux} @f$ values (degrees of freedom) per volume.
  The @f$ Q_{out} @f$ however contains only the @f$ K_{pde} + K_{aux} @f$ values, too, but the @f$ K_{aux} @f$ material parameters do not evolve in time and therefore are the same as in @f$ Q_{in} @f$.
- Besides @f$ Q_{in} @f$ and @f$ Q_{out} @f$ a kernel is given the current time @f$ t @f$ and the time step size @f$ \Delta t @f$.
- We never write a kernel for only one patch. We always write kernels that advance multiple patches in one go.
  So the "update one patch" is a special case.

## Data structures

Each Finite Volume solver embeds @f$N \times N@f$ patches
into the octants (cells of the spacetree).
A kernel reads the patch of one cell and never the patch of a neighbour
cell directly. However, we can use the faces.

We embed a @f$ 2N@f$ @f$ (d = 2) @f$ or
@f$2N^2 @f$ @f$ (d=3) @f$, respectively, patches into each face. Let
this auxiliary patch overlap the adjacent cells. Then we have a halo of
one cell available within each cell: We know the cell
data. We also have access to the @f$ 2d @f$ faces where each hosts a
degenerated patch. One layer of this patch is a copy of our own data,
i.e. does not give us additional information. The other layer of the
auxiliary patch however holds data from the neighbour. It gives us
information from the neighbour patch.


### Keeping face and patch data consistent

Projecting the patches onto the face halos and back is a mechanical task the
solver family exahype2::fv::Solver runs as dispatches: every cell writes the
boundary layers of its updated patch into its own half of its @f$2d@f$ faces,
the halo exchange carries the halves of the exchanged faces, and every level
jump interpolates the coarse half onto its fine faces and averages the fine
halves onto its coarse face.

@image html documentation/exahype2/solvers/ProjectPatchOntoFaces.png

The image above illustrates the projection: the dimensions of the patch and
of the face halo fix which layer of the cell lands in which half of a face.


## Code organisation

The @ref page_exahype2_blockstructured "patch operations" offer the
enumeration, the interpolation and the restriction the kernels read.


On the Python API side, the class `exahype2.fv.Solver` describes a Finite
Volume solver.
The scheme of a solver, `exahype2.fv.Godunov` or `exahype2.fv.MusclHancock`,
picks the reconstruction and the time stepping of the compute kernel. Every
Finite Volume solver advances its patches in one sequence of dispatches per
time step: while the halo messages travel the boundary faces, the local level
jumps and the local cells; then the remote level jumps, the remote cells, the
face projection and the restriction of the level jumps.

## Implementation

Our discussion of an implementation of the scheme - which can be read as
blueprint of our compute kernels in ExaHyPE - is inspired by

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
@article{10.1145/3406835,
author = {Uphoff, Carsten and Bader, Michael},
title = {Yet Another Tensor Toolbox for Discontinuous Galerkin Methods and Other Applications},
year = {2020},
issue_date = {December 2020},
publisher = {Association for Computing Machinery},
address = {New York, NY, USA},
volume = {46},
number = {4},
issn = {0098-3500},
url = {https://doi.org/10.1145/3406835},
doi = {10.1145/3406835},
journal = {ACM Trans. Math. Softw.},
month = oct,
articleno = {34},
numpages = {40},
keywords = {ADER-DG, Tensor operations, finite element method, high-performance computing}
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


who write down the algorithm as a sequence of tensor multiplications, i.e. generalised matrix-matrix equations.
This does not work in ExaHyPE 1:1 as we support non-linear PDEs in the generic kernel formulations.
However, we can use similar operator concatenation ideas.


We demonstrate the data flow by means of the simplified numerical flux which only takes the average.
This algorithm consists of a strict sequence of steps:

- We evaluate @f$ S(Q) @f$ per voxel. The output is stored in a @f$ p \times p \times p @f$ patch where each volume holds @f$ K_{pde} @f$ values.
  The output is called @f$ Q_{S} @f$.
- We evaluate @f$ F(Q)_x @f$ per voxel. That is the flux in one direction. The output is stored in a @f$ (p+2N) \times p \times p @f$ patch where each volume holds @f$ K_{pde} @f$ values.
  The output is called @f$ Q_{F_x} @f$. It is the core patch extended by the halo in one direction.
- We evaluate @f$ F(Q)_y @f$ per voxel. That is the flux in one direction. The output is stored in a @f$ p \times (p+2N) \times p @f$ patch where each volume holds @f$ K_{pde} @f$ values.
  The output is called @f$ Q_{F_y} @f$. It is the core patch extended by the halo in one direction.
- We evaluate @f$ F(Q)_z @f$ per voxel. That is the flux in one direction. The output is stored in a @f$ p \times p \times (p+2N) @f$ patch where each volume holds @f$ K_{pde} @f$ values.
  The output is called @f$ Q_{F_z} @f$. It is the core patch extended by the halo in one direction.

Now all temporary data are available. We prepare the output and then add the
PDE contributions. This part is a stencil variant. Or we can write it
down as a tensor-vector product which is added to @f$ Q_{out} @f$:

- We copy over @f$ Q_{in} @f$ into @f$ Q_{out} @f$. Per voxel, we have to copy @f$ K_{pde} + K_{aux} @f$ unknowns.
- Add to each voxel @f$ Q_{in}[x,y,z] @f$ the value @f$ \Delta t \cdot Q_{S}[x,y,z] @f$. This loop runs over @f$ K_{pde} @f$ unknowns only. The @f$ K_{aux} @f$ entries are not touched.
- Add to each voxel @f$ Q_{in}[x,y,z] @f$ the value @f$ \frac{\Delta t}{2h} \cdot (Q_{F_x}[x-1,y,z]+Q_{F_x}[x,y,z]+Q_{F_x}[x,y,z]+Q_{F_x}[x+1,y,z])@f$. This loop runs over @f$ K_{pde} @f$ unknowns only. The @f$ K_{aux} @f$ entries are not touched.
- Add to each voxel @f$ Q_{in}[x,y,z] @f$ the value @f$ \frac{\Delta t}{2h} \cdot (Q_{F_y}[x,y-1,z]+2Q_{F_y}[x,y,z]+Q_{F_y}[x,y+1,z])@f$. This loop runs over @f$ K_{pde} @f$ unknowns only. The @f$ K_{aux} @f$ entries are not touched.
- Add to each voxel @f$ Q_{in}[x,y,z] @f$ the value @f$ \frac{\Delta t}{2h} \cdot (Q_{F_z}[x,y,z-1]+2Q_{F_z}[x,y,z]+Q_{F_z}[x,y,z+1])@f$. This loop runs over @f$ K_{pde} @f$ unknowns only. The @f$ K_{aux} @f$ entries are not touched.

The extension to Rusanov for example is straightforward, as long as we take into account that our "stencil" here has to incorporate the max function.
Below is a schematic illustration of the data flow:

@image html documentation/exahype2/numerics/data-flow-finite-volumes.png

The scheme as well as the description lack one detail:
Every single step is to be done over a set of patches, as we update multiple patches in one go.
In the kernels, we write down this logic as a sequence of operators applied to the whole set of patches in one sweep.

*/
